## 7. Federation and Workforce Access

### Federation Model

    Corporate identity provider authenticates employee
      ↓
    IAM Identity Center evaluates account assignments
      ↓
    Permission set defines target-account access
      ↓
    Provisioned target-account IAM role
      ↓
    Federated temporary role session

- Centralized federation avoids creating an IAM user for every employee in
  every AWS account.
- It centralizes employee authentication, MFA, onboarding, and offboarding.
- AWS authorization remains responsible for deciding which account and role
  access the authenticated employee receives.

### SAML and OIDC Awareness

- SAML federation: IdP supplies a SAML assertion used with
  `AssumeRoleWithSAML` to obtain temporary credentials.
- OIDC federation: OIDC token can be used with `AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity`.

### IAM Identity Center

- Provides a centralized AWS access portal.
- Can use synchronized corporate users and groups.
- Multiple visible accounts remain separate accounts.
- Corporate groups are not AWS IAM groups:
  - Corporate groups contain workforce identities and drive account/
    permission-set assignments.
  - IAM groups contain IAM users and contribute IAM permissions.

### Permission Sets and Account Assignments

- Permission set defines permissions and session configuration centrally.
- Account assignment links a user or group, permission set, and target account.
- Identity Center provisions a corresponding IAM role into the target account.
- Federated employees receive temporary sessions for that role, not permanent
  target-account IAM users.

### Disabling a Federated User

- Prevents future authentication and new session creation.
- Does not inherently invalidate every already-issued AWS session.
- Existing sessions may continue until expiration unless revoked or blocked by
  another AWS-side authorization control.

