## 14. RBAC, ABAC, and Session Tags

### RBAC vs ABAC

RBAC:

- Authorization derives from assigned role or group.
- Easy to reason about but can create role proliferation as combinations grow.

ABAC:

- Authorization derives from attributes on the caller/session and resource.
- Core comparison:

    aws:PrincipalTag/Department
    == aws:ResourceTag/Department

- A single policy can cover many identities and resources when tags are
  governed consistently.

### Session Tags

- Added at role-assumption time and exposed as principal tags.
- Can represent department, project, environment, tenant, or cost center.
- Tags marked transitive can survive role chaining.
- Non-transitive tags disappear at the next hop.

### Evaluation and Governance

- Matching ABAC condition: Allow statement may apply.
- Mismatch: That Allow does not apply; result is implicit deny if no other Allow
  exists.
- Explicit Deny still overrides a matching tag condition.
- ABAC depends on tag integrity. A principal that can modify resource tags or
  supply arbitrary session tags may manipulate authorization.
- Restrict role/resource tagging and constrain permitted session-tag keys and
  transitive keys in trust-policy conditions.

